\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Labour says its wording was approved by the Commons clerks (who have to decide if a motion is in order). They also say there is precedent for including criticism of the PM in a no-confidence motion. In 1965 MPs voted on a motion saying: \u8220 That this house has no confidence in Her Majesty\u8217 s government and deplores the prime minister\u8217 s conduct of the nation\u8217 s affairs.\u8221  Harold Wilson was PM at the time, and he won by 13 votes.}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 But the government says that, since Boris Johnson has already said he is resigning as party leader, debating what would be effectively be a no-confidence motion in him, and not the government, is a waste of time. (In 1965 Wilson had not resigned as party leader.) A government spokesperson said:}
